<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Android has refused plain http:// by default since API 28, and the whole
    point of this app is a plain http:// laptop two feet away with no
    certificate and no domain name. Without this the app installs, opens, and
    then fails every request with a cleartext error that means nothing to
    anybody standing at a stall.

    This is deliberately a blanket base-config rather than a scoped one. The
    scoped version would be better, but <domain> takes a hostname or a literal
    IP address — it does not take a CIDR range — and the laptop's address is
    whatever the hotspot handed out that morning. "192.168.0.0/16" is not
    something this file can express, and hardcoding one address would break the
    moment the network changed, which is precisely when nobody has time to
    rebuild an APK.

    The cost is bounded: this app talks to one address, the one typed into its
    own "find the kitchen" screen, and it has no business on the public
    internet at all.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
